<p>Global oil prices surged above $100 a barrel as the US-Israel war with Iran entered its second week and traders priced in longer disruptions to Middle East supply and shipping routes. Several outlets said rising risk around the Strait of Hormuz—through which a large share of the world’s seaborne crude normally transits—was a key driver of the spike. The jump in crude rippled through financial markets, with reports of stock sell-offs and expectations that gasoline prices would rise, especially in the United States. President Trump defended the decision to go to war, calling higher oil prices a “very small price to pay” and arguing prices would fall once what he described as the “Iran nuclear threat” is removed. In a potentially stabilizing step, reports said G7 finance ministers planned to discuss a coordinated release of emergency oil reserves with the International Energy Agency, and that some members supported the idea.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/h12zY1RGCsizqevha_n2YkGrqfBOVXEctyaVULwAX2N7nbLVTDo-j7cIlIeWLbhY0X1yJRRPJK2h8NJPsppuAEAXoeJSVroroo7aK3XLkB_UOJ03HEquV-hr9XO_dk6JwB9lvyJD_UTkBAwbhwO0ibkPsvb4cb6AL06b87S9akT2fdT6oCZiGQKS4iJzlzK_n7HulzhF1IpOZ7FzathwvDs' alt='G7 finance ministers, who reports say will discuss releasing emergency oil reserves.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Market moves: Brent crude traded around $101.19 in early sessions after resuming on the Chicago Mercantile Exchange, up 9.2% from Friday’s settlement, while the US benchmark WTI was around $107.06.</li><li>Hormuz chokepoint: Rystad Energy estimated roughly 15 million barrels of crude—about 20% of global oil—move through the Strait of Hormuz each day, showing how quickly threats to traffic there can tighten markets.</li><li>Supply curbs: Iraq, Kuwait and the UAE cut oil production as the conflict complicated shipments and storage logistics, according to reports.</li><li>Fuel price signal: AAA data cited in reporting showed the US national average for regular gasoline up 15% over the past week to $3.45 per gallon, with some areas seeing nearly 30% increases.</li><li>Intraday highs: Reuters, cited in reporting, said Brent climbed as high as $111.04 a barrel while WTI touched $111.24 in early trading; later updates put both closer to the $106–$108 range.</li></ul><blockquote>Short term oil prices, which will drop rapidly when the destruction of the Iran nuclear threat is over, is a very small price to pay for U.S.A., and World, Safety and Peace. ONLY FOOLS WOULD THINK DIFFERENTLY! - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Donald Trump: He argued the oil-price spike would be temporary and worth the cost, framing the war as necessary for “world safety and peace.” (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/mar/09/iran-war-drives-oil-price-above-100-a-barrel-for-first-time-since-2022'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>US Energy Secretary Chris Wright: He told CNN he expected disruptions to be short-lived, saying the “worst case” would be “a few weeks,” not months. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/03/09/trump-defends-iran-war-decision-as-oil-soars-above-100_6751227_4.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li><li>Market analysts/traders (reported): Traders were described as betting that the widening conflict could trigger a weeks-long supply disruption, helping push crude above $100. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/56a01aa5-98af-48f0-b580-89e7bb4f59f6'>Financial Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/3/9/oil-soars-past-100-a-barrel-amid-iran-war'>Oil soars past $100 a barrel, stocks plunge as US-Israel war on Iran rages</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.boston.com/news/politics/2026/03/08/crude-oil-prices-surpass-100-a-barrel-as-the-iran-war-impedes-production-and-shipping/'>Crude oil prices surpass $100 a barrel as the Iran war impedes production and shipping</a> - boston.com</li><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/03